---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import register from "../data/register.json";
const rows = register.filter((r) => (r.surname || "").toUpperCase() === "CRISCI");
const swept = rows.filter((r) => r.kind === "swept").length;
---
<Base title="Crisci"
      eyebrow="The family the Falco married eleven times"
      dek="Generations one and three both married a Crisci. So did five Falco daughters, in the other direction — and the last of them married one in Australia.">

  <div class="card" style="margin-bottom:1.2rem">
    <p><strong>Crisci is not an in-law name. It is half the family.</strong> The archive can count
    <strong>eleven marriages</strong> between the two surnames across roughly two hundred years — six
    Crisci women marrying Falco men, five Falco women marrying Crisci men. It stands at the very head
    of this line, and it is still there in the Brisbane generation. It had no page.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Twice in the spine</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Gen</th><th>The marriage</th><th>How the archive knows</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="mono"><strong>1</strong></td>
        <td><strong>FRANCESCA CRISCI</strong> × <strong>Matteo Falco</strong> — the founding couple</td>
        <td>Named as the parents in <strong>three separate children's death acts</strong>: Angela in
        1822, Rosa in 1830, Pasquale in 1834. No act names Francesca's own parents.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="mono"><strong>3</strong></td>
        <td><strong>ANDREANA CRISCI</strong> × <strong>Vincenzo Falco</strong></td>
        <td>Named as a parent of Raffaele Falco, and placed on <strong>strada Camellara</strong>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>And a Falco daughter married out, on the same street, in the same years</h2>
  <p class="lede"><strong>Anna Falco</strong> — a daughter of Pasquale Falco and Chiara Rivetti, so a
  sister of generation three — married <strong>Arcangelo Crisci</strong> and lived on
  <strong>strada Camellara</strong>. She died there on 23 August 1820, aged twenty-five. Seven months
  earlier the couple had buried a two-day-old son, <strong>Fortunato Crisci</strong>, on the same
  street.</p>
  <p>This is what closed an old question here. The archive had two men called <strong>Arcangelo
  Crisci</strong> — one aged fifty-two and one aged thirty, both declaring births in 1817 — and could
  not tell which was which. Anna's act settles that <em>the younger one married the line's own
  daughter</em>. <strong>The Crisci married into this family twice in a single generation</strong>:
  Andreana marrying in, Anna marrying out.</p>

  <h2>The eleven crossings</h2>
  <p class="small">Six Crisci women in, five Falco women out. Dates where the archive has them.</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Crisci</th><th>married</th><th>Falco</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Francesca Crisci</strong></td><td class="mono">→</td><td><strong>Matteo Falco</strong> — generation one</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Andreana Crisci</strong></td><td class="mono">→</td><td><strong>Vincenzo Falco</strong> — generation three</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Alessandra Crisci <span class="mono small">b. 1817, d. 14 Nov 1869</span></td><td class="mono">→</td><td>Matteo Falco <span class="small">(the later Matteo, b. 1810)</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Andreana Crisci <span class="mono small">b. 1852</span></td><td class="mono">→</td><td>Gioacchino / Guglielmo Falco</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Chiara Crisci</td><td class="mono">→</td><td>Pasquale Falco</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Lelia Crisci</td><td class="mono">→</td><td>Giuseppe Falco</td></tr>
      <tr><td colspan="3" class="small" style="background:var(--ground)"><strong>and in the other direction —</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Arcangelo Crisci</td><td class="mono">←</td><td><strong>Anna Falco</strong> <span class="mono small">d. 23 Aug 1820, strada Camellara</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td>Aniello Crisci <span class="mono small">12 Nov 1807 – 25 Nov 1884</span></td><td class="mono">←</td><td>Giovanna Falco</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Antonio Crisci <span class="mono small">b. 1825</span></td><td class="mono">←</td><td>Filomena Falco</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Raffaele Crisci <span class="mono small">b. 1811</span></td><td class="mono">←</td><td>Francesca Falco <span class="mono small">1813 – 8 Jun 1876</span></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Pasquale Crisci</strong></td><td class="mono">←</td><td><strong>Costanza Falco</strong> — <em>daughter of Carminantonio and Filomena Annecchino</em></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <div class="card" style="margin:1.1rem 0">
    <p><strong>The last row is the one to sit with.</strong> Costanza Falco, sister of the Carmine
    Falco who married Francesca Zampiello, married a <strong>Pasquale Crisci</strong> — the same two
    surnames, and one of the same forenames, that stand at the head of this line in the 1780s. Whether
    there is any descent between the two is <strong>not known and not claimed</strong>. In a town
    where both names were common, recurrence is what you would expect. It is set down because it is
    true, not because it means anything yet.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Crisci on the ground</h2>
  <p>{swept} of these {rows.length} records were read off the Arienzo death registers act by act.
  They put Crisci households on <strong>strada Camellara</strong> — the Falco street — and across the
  town. Two things they show:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A Crisci × Morgillo couple of the founding generation.</strong> The 1843 register
    buries <strong>Fortunato Crisci, 59</strong>, born about 1784, <em>«figlio di PASQUALE CRISCI
    defunto … e di ANNA MORGILLO defunta»</em> — the two surnames that flank this family, in one
    household, in the right decade. <strong>Nothing links it to the Falco</strong>, and the archive
    says so on the page where it is recorded.</li>
    <li><strong>Crisci married into the Annecchino too.</strong> At Forchia, <strong>Giovanna
    Crisci</strong> married a <strong>Carmine Annecchino</strong> — so the name touches
    <a href={u("/annecchino")}>generation seven's line</a> as well as generations one and three.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The reading that is still open</h2>
  <p>One Crisci household is <strong>not settled</strong> and is published in that state. The 1823
  register gives <em>«Marcello Crisci &amp; Maria Rosa Mauro»</em>; the 1828 register gives
  <em>«Marcello <strong>Cossi</strong>»</em> with the identical wife. This archive has already been
  caught once by exactly that pair of shapes — five years of <em>Cossi</em> turned out to be
  <em>Cioffi</em> — so the doubt is left standing rather than resolved by preference. The full account
  is under <a href={u("/method")}>Method</a>.</p>

  <p class="small" style="margin-top:1.2rem">All {rows.length} Crisci records are listed with their
  sources at <a href={u("/register") + "?q=Crisci"}>the register →</a></p>
</Base>
